{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts following an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single expert because it replaces 'stochastic intuition' with 'deterministic verification'.","counterconditions":["The task requires synthesis of non-linear variables not captured by the checklist","The checklist is so comprehensive it induces 'compliance fatigue' (SCR_004)","The non-experts lack the baseline training to interpret the checklist's binary prompts"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition, while fast, is subject to 'availability bias' and 'cognitive tunneling' where a recognized pattern overrides contradictory evidence. The checklist forces a sequential scan of the entire problem space, ensuring that 'low-probability but high-impact' variables are explicitly evaluated. Reliability is gained via the reduction of omission errors (Type II) which are the primary failure mode of intuition.","optional_refs":["e55c3de6477bd8f3ad420cd44cb871ccd799098c2a71b8d427415fd30759468c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Reliability gains are genuine only when the panel exhibits 'difficulty-correlated cognitive friction' during the evaluation process.","counterconditions":["The panel is highly experienced with the checklist, leading to 'automaticity' that mimics compliance","The time-spike is caused by confusion or interpersonal conflict rather than rigorous verification (SCR_010)"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Mere compliance is characterized by a uniform rate of checklist completion regardless of item complexity. Genuine reliability is evidenced by a 'time-spike' (SCR_001) on items that are objectively harder to verify. This indicates that the panel is engaging in active verification (reasoning) rather than passive marking (compliance). The causal link is that reliability requires the expenditure of cognitive effort proportional to the evidence-gathering difficulty of the specific check.","optional_refs":["4c982bb24af1e5ba9b8b2efaf3ffbe6d731eb9e0c880009b2cd6a1899fda908e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single domain expert is more reliable because 'compressed expertise' (intuition) acts as a high-dimensional filter that checklists cannot replicate.","counterconditions":["The task is a 'boundary case' where standard patterns are misleading (SRC_005)","The expert is suffering from overconfidence bias or burnout"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is not a guess but a rapid pattern-match against thousands of previous cases. A checklist decomposes this into a linear series of checks, which can lead to 'fragmentation error'—where the panel gets every individual check 'right' but fails to see the emergent systemic failure that the expert's holistic intuition would immediately flag. Reliability is lost when the sum of the parts is less than the whole.","optional_refs":["88e83df284a970d327702560c656f58dd7f1b0badf69c97cb47feb0ba6ad3d93","d61cc68c8fb9c612a7a7a0ae1bf95d9568bb41e30ffc5a40f152cd1c75ee5885"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The reliability gain of a panel is evidenced by the 'Causal Justification of Decoy Rejection'.","counterconditions":["The panel has been trained to expect decoys, turning the rejection into another form of compliance","The decoy is too subtle, leading to 'false positives' of reliability"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To distinguish reliability from compliance, a decoy item (irrelevant to the outcome) is inserted into the checklist. A compliance-driven panel will simply check the box. A reliability-driven panel will not only reject the decoy but provide a justification demonstrating a lack of causal link between the decoy and the verifiable outcome (SCR_016). This proves the panel is evaluating the *logic* of the process rather than just following the *instructions* of the tool.","optional_refs":["b6e71d00f4fdbfb5e613c7dd643f0400c11e0743df3c8913bc34ca3a4c47e50a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Panel reliability is a function of 'Inter-Panel Variance' on difficult items, not binary correctness.","counterconditions":["The checklist is so prescriptive that it eliminates all variance regardless of reliability","The panels are subject to groupthink (SCR_011), artificially inflating agreement"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a compliance-driven system, multiple panels will show 100% agreement because they are all following the same script, even if the script is flawed. In a reliability-driven system, there will be variance on the most difficult checklist items (reflecting genuine cognitive struggle) but convergence on the correct final answer. The causal mechanism is that reliability emerges from the independent verification of evidence, which naturally produces variance in the *process* but stability in the *outcome*.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there a formal way to quantify the 'fragmentation error' where checklist compliance leads to a correct 'sum of parts' but a wrong 'whole'?","related_refs":["SCR_004"]},{"question":"Can we distinguish between 'rigorous verification' and 'total confusion' in the time-spike metric without knowing the answer beforehand?","related_refs":["SCR_001","SCR_010"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}